  "request_sha256": "66da57ed77df7d09e9dcc5b3da5808689278d6bc4954aaf87bdc8998db1901af",
  "response": {
    "data": {
      "A": "Research a low-overhead streaming row DP as the robust dense fallback. Replace the two DP rows and row copy with one array and a register holding the updated left neighbor. Initialize dp[0]=1 and all other entries to zero; reset the left value each row, and reset both values at blocked cells. Since two residues sum to at most 2*MOD-2, use addition and one conditional subtraction instead of %. Investigate compact row buckets with sorted obstacle columns, processing obstacle-free spans without a packed-bit lookup per cell. Preserve duplicate-coordinate semantics and avoid per-row allocations. Compare obstacle representations and faster input handling separately rather than assuming each helps. Target O(N^2) traversal with O(N+M) storage, correct for N=1 and blocked endpoints. Report whole-program measurements, including parsing and preprocessing.",
      "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle combinatorial algorithm. Deduplicate obstacles, reject blocked endpoints, and sort by (y,x). Precompute factorials and inverse factorials through 2*N-2, which is below MOD. For each obstacle and finally the destination, compute w[i]=C(y[i]+x[i],y[i]) minus contributions w[j]*C(dy+dx,dy) from earlier coordinate-wise reachable obstacles. This counts paths by their first blocked cell. Use bounded modular accumulation and 64-bit products. The obstacle-free case naturally reduces to C(2*N-2,N-1), not a stored answer. Target O(N+M log M+U^2) time and O(N+M) storage, where U is the distinct obstacle count. Retain a correct dense fallback: even sub-1% density can make U^2 prohibitive. Research conservative crossover decisions based on dimensions, distinct obstacle count, and measured costs—not test identities.",
      "C": "Research SIMD-friendly and four-core alternatives to the dependent row scan. Start with an anti-diagonal frontier: for d=x+y, each cell receives prev[x]+prev[x-1], with missing predecessors zero. Seed the origin separately and zero blocked destinations before the next diagonal consumes them. Cells within one diagonal are independent, allowing contiguous vectorized modular additions using two O(N) buffers. Bucket obstacles by diagonal without constructing an N-by-N mask. First measure a single-thread vectorizable implementation; then investigate persistent two- and four-worker execution using facilities supported by the unchanged build. Maintain explicit synchronization between dependent diagonals, zero boundaries correctly, and avoid duplicate-coordinate write races. If per-diagonal barriers dominate, investigate coarse tiled wavefronts with explicit top/left boundary dependencies and bounded frontier storage. Retain parallel execution only where measured gains exceed scheduling and memory-traffic costs.",
      "G": "Integrate only programs produced by A/B/C. First establish the strongest correct standalone candidate, then measure incremental combinations: shared parsing and deduplication, B's sparse dispatch, and A or C as the dense backend. Decide the algorithm before allocating backend-specific storage; avoid paying for multiple obstacle layouts unnecessarily. Derive dispatch thresholds from legitimate input properties and observed timings. Do not assume gains add: compare every integration against its constituent programs and keep a standalone winner if integration regresses. Use the unchanged compiler, target, budgets, oracle, and tests. Require correctness checks and explicit reasoning for N=1, empty grids, blocked endpoints, unordered or repeated obstacles, boundary cells, and modular overflow. Report complete-program timing and correctness evidence, not kernel-only speedups.",
      "analysis": "All roles concern Problem D, identified by src/solution.cpp; the other official problems are irrelevant. The baseline passed four correctness cases and averaged 0.0816945 seconds over three timing samples. No input dimensions or profiling are supplied, so bottlenecks remain hypotheses. The source performs O(N^2) DP, stores an O(N^2)-bit obstacle grid, copies each completed row, and uses default stream input. The ARM64 target has four CPU cores and no GPU. The research portfolio separates a low-risk scalar improvement, an algorithmic sparse-obstacle alternative, and dependency-aware SIMD/parallel execution. Sparse density alone does not justify quadratic work in the obstacle count. Every proposal must preserve exact modulo-1000000007 results for all valid inputs, without specialization to measured cases."
    },
